@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "History of Neovim";
    ViewData["Tag"] = "Learn";
    ViewData["Lead"] = "How a refactoring fork of Vim became the centre of a modern editing ecosystem.";
}

        <h2>Timeline</h2>
        <div class="timeline"><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">1976</div><div>vi is written by Bill Joy for Unix, bringing modal editing to terminals.</div></div><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">1991</div><div>Bram Moolenaar releases Vim (Vi IMproved), adding multi-level undo, syntax highlighting and plugins.</div></div><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">2014</div><div>Neovim is started as a refactoring fork of Vim to modernise the codebase, enable async jobs and embrace community contributions.</div></div><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">2019</div><div>Neovim 0.4 introduces floating windows, the foundation of many modern UI plugins.</div></div><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">2021</div><div>Neovim 0.5 ships a built-in LSP client, Treesitter support and Lua as a first-class configuration language.</div></div><div class="tl-item"><div class="tl-year">2023 &ndash; 2025</div><div>Releases 0.9, 0.10 and 0.11 refine the LSP client, add better defaults and improve the Lua API.</div></div></div>
        <h2>Vim vs Neovim</h2>
        <div class="table-responsive"><table class="table nv-table table-striped"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Vim</th><th>Neovim</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Config language</td><td>Vimscript (Vim9script)</td><td>Lua and Vimscript</td></tr><tr><td>Built-in LSP</td><td>No (plugins only)</td><td><span class="badge badge-nv-green">Yes</span></td></tr><tr><td>Treesitter</td><td>No</td><td><span class="badge badge-nv-green">Yes</span></td></tr><tr><td>Embedded terminal</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, with a richer API</td></tr><tr><td>Remote plugins / RPC</td><td>Limited</td><td>msgpack-RPC API for any language</td></tr><tr><td>Default config path</td><td><code>~/.vimrc</code></td><td><code>~/.config/nvim/init.lua</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>

